"""hearth-server-hub: the Home server helper's Game servers, Game cache, Remote play, Media and PCs pages
(docs/SERVER-HUB.md; boards docs/mockups/server-expansion/05, 07, 08, 09 and 10). It ships in Mantel's folder, so it
comes and updates with Mantel's Store part, never with a Hearth OS update. Everything it does as you is here;
everything that needs root (a system package, the games' firewall rule, the cache) goes through the Home server helper
(hearth-home-server-helper, a Store system part) or hearth-server-dns, each asking for the admin password once.

It builds on what Hearth already has and never copies it:
  games  hearth-server-apps (the game servers in your rootless Podman, their world backups) and a few more games:
         7 Days to Die, Ark and your own games' headless builds (Gravesleep, Noclip Protocol, the quad mech game)
  cache  hearth-server-dns (LANCache and Hearth Shield's redirects; its numbers in /run/hearth/server-dns)
  play   hearth-remote-desktop (Sunshine, pairing, who's streaming), Wake-on-LAN
  media  Jellyfin (hearth-home-server installs it), your own discs, the antenna through Jellyfin's Live TV, your own
         videos and public-domain films from the Internet Archive. Never anything else: no torrents, no searching for
         copies of things you don't own.
  pcs    your other Hearth PCs over Hearth Link (hearth-device-status): what each runs, its share and its limits

Your choices: ~/.config/nimbus/server-hub.json through hearth_settings_io (atomic, .bak, merge under a lock; only what
you change is written; defaults fill in when read and are never saved). What it saw (players each hour, streams,
moves): ~/.local/state/nimbus/server-hub.json. Nothing here starts by itself: its timer (hearth-server-hub.timer,
every 5 minutes) is enabled only while a schedule, Stop when nobody's on, Pause while you play or a streaming rule
is on, it never waits for anything at start-up, and while a game is being played on this PC it only does what the
rule asks (pausing), nothing heavy.

  hearth-server-hub view games|cache|play|media|pcs   JSON for that page
  hearth-server-hub set PART KEY JSON                 one choice (checked against what that page offers)
  hearth-server-hub game start|stop|restart|install ID
  hearth-server-hub game-console ID                   its last lines
  hearth-server-hub game-kick ID NAME                 where the game has a console for it (Minecraft)
  hearth-server-hub game-build ID                     your game's newest headless build in place, then restarted
  hearth-server-hub game-make ID                      where your game's server build is made (its repository's Actions)
  hearth-server-hub game-opt ID KEY VALUE             one of its choices (hearth-server-apps checks it)
  hearth-server-hub invite ID                         its Tailscale address and where to share it
  hearth-server-hub world-backup ID | world-restore ID FILE
  hearth-server-hub mod-add ID NAMESPACE-NAME | mod-set ID NAMESPACE-NAME on|off   Thunderstore mods (Valheim)
  hearth-server-hub schedule ID DAY FROM TO | schedule ID DAY off
  hearth-server-hub cache-size GB | cache-drive DIR | cache-install
  hearth-server-hub play-stop | play-message TEXT | play-wake | play-launch ID | play-pin PIN
  hearth-server-hub play-device DEVICE size|fps|bitrate VALUE
  hearth-server-hub media-want JSON | media-yes ID | media-pick ID TITLE | media-rip
  hearth-server-hub pcs-add NAME
  hearth-server-hub tick                               what the timer runs
Output: one JSON line; failures print "error: ..." and exit 1. No shell anywhere: every command is an argument list.

$HEARTH_SERVER_HUB_FIXTURE: a JSON file of what each source answers (CI's pictures and the tests); nothing changes then.
